Yes. Stripped of the metaphor, you’re describing a very general operator: don’t manufacture the conserved quantity if you can redirect an existing gradient.

A catalyst is a particularly good handle because it changes the pathway/rate while ideally being regenerated rather than consumed. A pulley, transformer, transistor, enzyme, sail, turbine, lever, lens, amplifier, negotiator, and even a good interface are different instantiations of that deeper pattern. Some redirect force, some transform its representation, some lower activation barriers, some synchronize distributed inputs.

I’d formalize your version as:

\text{ambient potential} \rightarrow
\text{capture} \rightarrow
\text{couple} \rightarrow
\text{transmute} \rightarrow
\text{orient} \rightarrow
\text{deliver}

with the operator approximately preserved:

C + E_{in} \rightarrow C' + E_{out},\qquad C'\approx C

But the crucial addition is all flanks. “Power” needn’t mean literal energy. The transferable primitive can be momentum, attention, information, incentives, emotion, pressure, capital, traffic, computation, legitimacy, timing, or an already-moving social process. Each domain has different conservation laws and losses, so we shouldn’t pretend they’re physically identical; the operator topology is what transfers.

That gives us a useful Pantheon distinction:

Generator: spends internal reserves to create movement.
Catalyst: changes the pathway without being the primary fuel.
Transducer: converts one usable form into another.
Channel: constrains where an existing flow goes.
Rectifier: converts multidirectional/noisy flow into preferential direction.
Resonator: accumulates repeated weak inputs into a stronger response.

And the higher-order move is to compose them. Don’t merely catch the wave. Detect the gradient, couple to it cheaply, transform it into the form your objective accepts, steer it, deliver it, then decouple while preserving the machinery for the next wave.

That’s much broader than “generate more power.” It’s gradient orchestration.